  "Analysis": [
    {
      "predicate": "iadas:typeOfAnalysis",
      "object": "Simple linear regression"
    },
    {
      "predicate": "iadas:authorConclusion",
      "object": "Variables such as sex, age, BMI and body dissatisfaction explained 52% of the variance. Body dissatisfaction was the strongest predictor. Sports related variables were able to account for an additional 9% of variance in disorded eating. The most influential was the desire to be leaner to improve sports performance. General body dissatisfaction, sports-related body dissatisfaction was not a significant predictor for disordered eating. General and sports-related body dissatisfaction shared 37% of variance."
    },
    {
      "predicate": "iadas:limites",
      "object": "Our sample size was too small to test for differences if gender was included in the analyzes as well. The EAT-26 was developed with non-athletic samples. The assessment of body dissatisfaction needs to be discussed critically. We opted for measures that focus on body fat and left out the dimension of muscularity. Nonetheless, muscularity might be important as to how athletes perceive their ideal body and more valid especially in male athletes. For ATHLETES questionnaires, limitations result from adapting and applying the questuonnaire within our sample."
    },
    {
      "predicate": "iadas:perspectives",
      "object": "Future studies could apply more differentiated assessments of disordered eating which allow analyzing if risk factors are the same for restricted and bulimic eating patterns. \nAchievement goal theory appears very promising for further studies that seek to understand the different risks for eating disorders in athletes. Longitudinal and experimental investigations are necessary to test whether the sports-related correlates of disordered eating tested in our study constitute risk factors for disordered eating and whether the assumed causal direction can be confirmed. A comparison between sport types regarding risk factors could help to explain why the risk for disordered eating is higher in certain sport types."
    },
